  <section class="broadsheet-panel" aria-label="Excerpt: Consider the tree">
    <div class="broadsheet-masthead">
      <span>Excerpt</span>
      <span>Consider the tree</span>
      <span>opening</span>
    </div>

    <h2 class="broadsheet-title" style="font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3.5vw, 2.6rem);">A consultant's natural history of the knot.</h2>

    <div class="broadsheet-body">
      <p>Trees are very old. They have been doing what they do for a long time, and the doing has gotten quite refined. A branch begins as a reach for light. If the reach succeeds, it pulls more wood toward itself; if it fails, the tree closes around the failed reach and keeps growing. We call what remains a knot.</p>

      <p>A knot is a record. It is the place where a piece of the past sits inside the present, load-bearing or not depending on how it was sealed. Carpenters know this. So does anyone who has tried to change an old institution.</p>

      <p>I work with knots. Most of my consulting is about helping people read what is already there &mdash; the prior reach, the prior failure, the wood that closed around both &mdash; before they decide what to do next.</p>

      <p class="broadsheet-pull">"Heartwood is dead but load-bearing."</p>

      <p>The body of a tree is mostly inert. Cells that did the work of conduction die in place and become the structural column the next year's growth depends on. This is not a metaphor I find useful so much as a fact I keep returning to. Living things are scaffolded by their dead parts. The work is to know which dead parts.</p>

      <p>Read the full essay at <a href="/consider-the-tree/">/consider-the-tree/</a>.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="broadsheet-folio">
      <span>Folio II</span>
      <span>&larr; back &middot; forward &rarr;</span>
    </div>
  </section>

